// ============================================================================
== 5.1 What a Coup Is

A *coup d'état* is the sudden, illegal seizure of power by a small group, usually from
within the armed forces, using the machinery of the state rather than a mass movement. It
is not a *revolution*, which changes the social order and involves large numbers of
people, and it is not a *mutiny*, which is a refusal of orders inside the military without
the aim of taking the government. The word that matters in the definition is *illegal*: a
coup succeeds by breaking the constitutional order, and every military government in
Nigeria therefore began by suspending the constitution it had sworn to defend.

Nigeria had six successful coups and several failed ones. The first, on 15 January 1966,
was led by Major Chukwuma Kaduna Nzeogwu and other young officers; it killed the Prime
Minister, Sir Abubakar Tafawa Balewa, the Premier of the Northern Region, Sir Ahmadu Bello,
and the Premier of the Western Region, Chief Samuel Akintola, and it ended with the
Commander of the Army, Major-General J. T. U. Aguiyi-Ironsi, as head of the federal
military government. A counter-coup on 29 July 1966 killed Ironsi and brought
Lieutenant-Colonel Yakubu Gowon to power.

#trap-box("Coup, counter-coup and attempted coup are three different answers")[
  The paper likes to test the words. A *coup* removes a government and puts the plotters in power. A *counter-coup* removes the government that the first coup installed. An *attempted coup* fails and leaves the government in place — the Dimka attempt of 13 February 1976 is the classic Nigerian example, and it succeeded only in killing Murtala Muhammed, after which Obasanjo took over. If the question says “the coup that brought Gowon to power”, the answer is the July 1966 counter-coup, not January.
]

== 5.2 Why Soldiers Intervene

Five explanations recur in the textbooks, and the paper expects the candidate to name
several rather than to give one. The first is *corruption and the failure of politicians*:
the First Republic's leaders were accused of using office for private gain, and the coup
plotters of 1966 justified themselves in exactly those words. The second is *electoral
fraud*: the 1964 federal election and the 1965 Western Region election were both followed
by violence, and the army presented itself as the only institution able to restore order.
The third is *ethnic and sectional politics*: the parties were regional, the census was
disputed, and the soldiers from one region feared a government dominated by another. The
fourth is *economic mismanagement*: inflation, unemployment and the collapse of the
marketing-board surpluses gave the coup a public willing to applaud it. The fifth is the
*weakness of civilian institutions* — a parliament that could not control the executive, a
judiciary that could not punish the powerful and a police force that could not keep order
left the army as the only organised alternative.

