== 5.4 How Military Government Ruled

Military government is government by decree. A *decree* is a law made by the head of state
without a legislature, and it takes effect from the date it names, sometimes retrospectively.
Because the courts could otherwise test a decree against the constitution, military
governments inserted an *ouster clause*: a provision saying that no court has jurisdiction
to inquire into the decree or into anything done under it. They established *special
military tribunals* to try civilians for offences such as treason and currency trafficking,
with no appeal to the ordinary courts. They *suspended the constitution*, dissolved the
legislatures and the elected councils, banned political parties, detained opponents without
trial, and controlled the press — Decree No. 4 of 1984 made the publication of false
information about a public officer an offence, and it was used against journalists.

