    A coup is the sudden, illegal seizure of power by a small group, usually soldiers; a counter-coup removes the government the first coup installed, and an attempted coup fails. Nigeria's soldiers intervened because politicians were corrupt, elections were rigged, politics was sectional, the economy was strained and civilian institutions were too weak to correct any of it. The order runs Ironsi, Gowon, Murtala, Obasanjo, Shagari, Buhari, Babangida, Shonekan, Abacha and Abubakar. Military government ruled by decree, protected its decrees with ouster clauses, tried civilians before special tribunals, suspended the constitution and detained opponents without trial. It also abolished the regions, created states from twelve in 1967 to thirty-six in 1996, moved the capital to Abuja, reformed local government and helped found ECOWAS. Civilian rule returned on 1 October 1979 and again on 29 May 1999, and the lesson the paper wants is that the army centralised the country while destroying the habit of constitutional government.
